    {
      "id": "s2-intro",
      "type": "intro",
      "label": null,
      "title": "BOL4 — Some",
      "content": "Setting the scene before your main point. Extremely common in spoken Korean.\n\nThis song is a direct celebration of 썸 (sseom) — the specifically Korean dating-culture stage before you're official, where you're texting constantly, hanging out one-on-one, and everyone around you can tell something's happening except neither of you has said the words. The singer is impatient and giddy about it: she's decided she's going to chase this, call every day, push past her own nerves, even though she's sure she'll get rejected.\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn the grammar pattern **-는데**, which means \"background info / mild contrast.\" We'll see how BOL4 uses it in \"Some.\"",
      "musicPlaying": true
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-intro",
      "type": "intro",
      "label": null,
      "title": "이번에 배울 것",
      "content": "이번 레슨에서 배울 단어:\n- **걷다** — to walk\n- **비** — rain\n- **비가 오는데** — it's raining, and...\n- **걷고 있는데** — I was walking, and...\n\n레슨이 끝나면, 이 노래의 가사를 이해할 수 있어요.",
      "musicPlaying": true
    },
